      \field{annotation}{Kelly, P., for the court; affirmed. Inhabitants of Clarendon petitioned the Circuit Court of Arlington County for a town charter under ch. 116 of the Code of 1919; the board of supervisors and several hundred county residents opposed it, and the court dismissed the petition. Holds, 132 Va. at 406, that where proposed corporate limits are "carved out of a thickly settled section" the "general good of the community" the statute requires "must be construed as embracing the whole section", following Board of Supervisors of Norfolk County v. Duke, 113 Va. 94. At 407: Clarendon and Ballston are "practically interlocked", about a mile apart, and "it appears doubtful whether any small town could be chartered therein without detriment to the general good of the county" - the passage samuel2026 note 7 reads as the inference that no town may incorporate within Arlington. The court did not call Arlington "a continuous, contiguous, and homogeneous community": neither "homogeneous" nor "homogenous" nor "contiguous" occurs anywhere in the opinion, and its own words are "a part only of a thickly settled community". The attribution is rose1976 p. 176, which puts the phrase in quotation marks in the court's mouth and cites nothing for it; she reuses it at p. 197 of the 1932 change and at p. 229 of 1976. bestebreurtje2017 quotes her p. 197 sentence, and her own account of this case, at p. 14 n. 25, is accurate and free of the phrase. Filed in Drive as "legal/state courts/Supreme Court of Appeals of Virginia 1922 - Bennett v. Garrett.pdf", the Harvard Caselaw Access Project scan of 132 Va. 397 as CourtListener serves it, saved 30 September 2026}
