      \field{labeltitlesource}{title}
      \field{annotation}{Spencer, J. Same docket (No. CL07-2426-4) as a July 2007 ruling on a demurrer, reported separately at 73 Va. Cir. 471, 2007 Va. Cir. LEXIS 136; this is the later ruling, on cross-motions for summary judgment, where the holding sits. The Mayor had declared Council's own legislative-staff appointments (Council Liaisons, its Chief of Staff, Policy Analyst, Public Relations Specialist and Budget Analyst, the Director of Legislative Services, and the City Assessor) void, and the Deputy Chief Administrative Officer had told those employees to reapply for their jobs. Richmond is a mayor-council city with an appointed Chief Administrative Officer, not a County Manager Plan locality, and its Charter sec. 5.03 carries the non-interference language this report's search was for: "the mayor, council, and its members shall deal with the administrative services solely through the chief administrative officer, and neither the mayor, council, nor any member thereof shall give orders ... to any subordinate of the chief administrative officer." Holds that sec. 5.03's "ultimate responsibility" sentence gives the CAO authority over administrative and executive branch employees only, read together with secs. 5.02 and 5.04 and with the separation-of-powers clause of the Virginia Constitution's Art. III sec. 1, so Council keeps the implied power under its Charter sec. 4.02 to appoint the legislative staff it needs to do its own legislative work; cites the Dillon's Rule holding of \textit{Commonwealth v. Arlington County Bd.}, 217 Va. 558, 574 (1977), the same case cited here as commonwealthvarlington1977. Summary judgment for Council. Neither opinion under this docket cites \textit{County Board of Arlington County v. Brown}, and CourtListener, Justia and a general web search turn up no appeal of either ruling to the Court of Appeals of Virginia or the Supreme Court of Virginia. Filed in Drive as "legal/state courts/Richmond City Circuit Court 2007 - City of Richmon