      \field{annotation}{Riely, J.; reversed. Winchester's common council had offered a standing $500 reward for the apprehension and conviction of incendiaries, and Redmond, having secured two convictions, sued the city for it. Holds, 93 Va. at 714, quoting Dillon on Municipal Corporations sec. 89 (3rd ed.), that a municipal corporation has only "those granted in express words", those "necessarily or fairly implied in or incident to the powers expressly granted", and those "essential to the declared objects and purposes of the corporation, not simply convenient, but indispensable", and that "Any fair, reasonable doubt concerning the existence of power is resolved by the courts against the corporation, and the power is denied." The city had no such power: crime is an offence against the State, and the charter's general clause letting the council "do all such things as it may deem proper for the prosperity, quiet, and good order of the city" is confined to "the ordinary objects and purposes of municipal corporations". schragger2022 note 79 names this as the case in which the court "directly quoted what we know as Dillon's Rule". Filed in Drive as "legal/state courts/Supreme Court of Appeals of Virginia 1896 - City of Winchester v. Redmond.pdf", the Harvard Caselaw Access Project scan of 93 Va. 711 as CourtListener serves it, saved 30 September 2026}
