      \field{annotation}{Ch. 198, Acts of the General Assembly of Virginia, session of 1952, printed pp. 226-228, approved 6 March 1952 [S 174]. The act that offered Arlington the strong-manager form and left the choice to the voters. Sec. 15-352 keeps the Manager's core grant, today's sec. 15.2-706 almost word for word: "The administrative and executive powers of the county, including the power of appointment of all officers and employees whose appointment or election is not otherwise provided by law, are vested in an official known as the county manager". Department heads, however, "shall be selected by the county board; provided that if a majority of the qualified voters voting in the election required by § 15-355.1 vote in favor thereof, then the heads of the several county departments ... shall be appointed by the county manager." New sec. 15-352.2 sets the Manager's term at one year - it "shall expire on June thirtieth of each year after nineteen hundred fifty-two", with sixty days' notice if his services are not wanted, and removal only "for neglect of duty, malfeasance or misfeasance in office, or incompetency" - unless the referendum carries, after which he is "appointed for an indefinite period and be subject to removal by the county board at any time", with a written statement of reasons and a public hearing if he asks, suspension capped at thirty days, and the Board's action "not be subject to review". New sec. 15-355.1 prints the two questions for 1952: "Shall the county manager be appointed for an indefinite term, subject to removal at any time by the county board?" and "Shall the heads of the county departments be appointed by the county manager?", with sec. 15-360.1 (vaacts1952c49) supplying the machinery. Both carried on 4 November 1952 (dailysun1952results). Sec. 15-353 is amended on the budget: the Manager prepares the tentative budget by 15 March, the Board may alter it only before 15 May, and if it does not act the Manager's budget stands. Filed