      \field{annotation}{Ch. 591, Acts of the General Assembly of Virginia, session of 1952, printed pp. 1035-1036, approved 3 April 1952 [H 650]. Board elections, offered to the voters and never petitioned onto the ballot. The act re-enacts sec. 15-351, and subsection (e) sets a hard window: a petition "signed by two hundred or more qualified voters" filed with the circuit court "on or before the first day of August, nineteen hundred fifty-two", a court order "on or before the first day of September, nineteen hundred fifty-two", and the vote "at the next regular election to be held in November, nineteen hundred fifty-two". Two questions were to be printed: "Shall the members of the county board be elected biennially for four year terms, as provided in subsection (f) of § 15-351 of the Code of Virginia?" and "Shall the membership on the county board be increased from five to seven?" Subsection (f) works out the staggering that a yes would have produced, and subsection (g) provides that on a yes to the second question "there shall be elected two additional members of the board, for terms of three years" at the November 1953 election. No petition was filed, so neither question reached the ballot and the window closed; the seven- member question reached Arlington voters in 1952 only as a conditional clause of the incorporation package, which failed (dailysun1952petitions, dailysun1952results). 1954 (vaacts1954c151) and 1958 (vaacts1958c207) reopened the window and dropped the board-size question. At-large election is not among the questions. Filed in Drive as "legal/state statutes/Commonwealth of Virginia 1952 - Acts and Joint Resolutions, Session of 1952 (ch. 591).pdf", the page text as HathiTrust umn.31951d02280223t serves it, set out on a plain page by code/sources/pages.py and printed on 1 October 2026; HathiTrust refuses an automated request, so no copy of the page image was had.}
