      \field{labeltitlesource}{title}
      \field{annotation}{Lewis, J. Seven men whom Petersburg's outgoing common council elected to city offices three days before eleven of its twenty-four members left office petitioned for mandamus to compel the city auditor to pay their salaries; the writs were refused, the ordinance under which they were elected being an unreasonable exercise of the council's power and the council's power of election already spent. Holds, 76 Va. at 961, citing Dillon on Municipal Corporations sec. 91 (3rd ed.), that "in the construction of charters of corporations, whether public or private, it is a settled rule of interpretation ... that only such powers can be exercised under them as are clearly comprehended within the words of the charter, or derived therefrom by necessary implication, regard being had to the objects of the grant. And any doubt arising out of the terms used by the legislature must be resolved in favor of the public." schragger2022 note 79 reads this as the point at which Virginia adopted Dillon's Rule in full, rejecting the contrary doctrine of Cooley that the court had followed in Ould & Carrington v. City of Richmond, 64 Va. 464 (1873). Filed in Drive as "legal/state courts/Supreme Court of Appeals of Virginia 1882 - Kirkham v. Russell.pdf", the Harvard Caselaw Access Project scan of 76 Va. 956 and the pages that follow it in the volume, as CourtListener serves it, saved 30 September 2026}
