      \field{annotation}{This is where "continuous, contiguous, and homogeneous community" comes from, and Rose gives no source for it. At p. 176, on Bennett v. Garrett: "The case was eventually decided by the Supreme Court of Appeals of Virginia which held that Arlington County was a 'continuous, contiguous, and homogeneous community' that could not be subdivided for the purposes of incorporating a portion of it." None of the three words occurs anywhere in the opinion, the page's only footnote is to Acts of Assembly 1920 ch. 241 for the county's renaming, and the quotation marks are Rose's own; see bennettvgarrett1922. At p. 197 she reuses the phrase for the change the Board made in 1932: "the fact that the Magisterial Districts had been abolished and the County Board elected at large, meant a governing body more responsive to the needs of the whole County rather than pitting one section against another. The 'Continuous, contiguous, and homogeneous' nature of Arlington had now found expression in its form of government." At p. 229 she uses it a third time, of 1976: Arlington is "much more 'continuous [and] contiguous' than fifty years ago, but far less 'homogeneous'." Her spelling throughout is homogeneous. bestebreurtje2017 note 552 prints homogenous and cites p. 167, where this printing carries a passage on rail lines and air travel; Arlington's own County Manager Plan page quotes the p. 197 sentence and spells it homogeneous. Pages read: the title page, 176 and 197, from the Internet Archive's borrowable scan on 30 September 2026. On 1 October 2026 the chapters covering the 1930s-50s (pp. 174-230: the end of Chapter VIII through Chapters IX-XII) were read in full against the same scan, in-book search run on "incorporating", "incorporate", "staggered", "1938", "1958", "1959" and "Commission". Neither incorporation-commission-1959 nor referendum-1938-canvass is answered here: Rose's narrative has nothing under those years on the Board's term structure or a study of incorporation. What p. 174-176 and 194-196 do carry is earlier and later material on the same incorporation question - an unnamed 1920s committee (Mackey, Douglas, Moncure, Finney, Jesse) that drafted a charter for "a city of the first class" and went nowhere, folded into the Bennett v. Garrett discussion already cited above; and the referendum of 4 November 1930 that adopted the County Manager Plan, where "a diversion was created by the desire of some elements to study the matter of incorporating the whole County as a city" before the Board chose instead to put the Manager Plan to a vote (2,067 for change of government to 1,031 against; 1,936 for the Manager Plan to 428 for a Modified Commission Plan; 1,689 for election at large to 1,149 for election by district). Neither passage is about 1958-59 or 1938, so neither is filed; the rest of the book (chapters I-VII, XIII on) is still unread. Filed in Drive as "books/Rose 1976 - Arlington County, Virginia - A History (title page and pp. 176, 197 excerpt).pdf", page images captured from the borrowed scan in Sally's own browser, images reduced to 150 dpi}
