      \field{annotation}{Read 30 September 2026. The Commission on Constitutional Revision's 1969 draft included the provision, quoted at 183 from the Commission's report, that "a charter county or a city may exercise any power or perform any function which is not denied to it by this Constitution, by its charter, or by laws enacted by the General Assembly"; it was dropped before the draft reached the voters in 1970. At 205, quoting the House of Delegates committee report: "The committee does not recommend a constitutional provision proposed by the Commission to reverse the so-called 'Dillon' rule of strict construction of municipal powers. The first sentence of Section 3 as introduced, therefore, has been deleted. The Municipal League also told the committee it was against this change because, as a matter of experience it felt the General Assembly had granted to cities the powers they need and desire." The opposition was the Virginia Municipal League's, described at 200 as "somewhat counterintuitive"; the Virginia Association of Counties' executive board had proposed the opposite in 1968, that localities be given every power not denied them (note 102), and reversed its later hostility only in its 2020 legislative program (note 174). At 195-96 and notes 79-80: the court took Dillon's canon of strict construction in kirkhamvrussell1882 and quoted it outright in winchestervredmond1896, and the rule "has now been the law of Virginia for 140 years". At 205-08 the authors place the rejection in the racial politics of the moment, Richmond turning majority-Black while white suburban and rural leaders were wary of empowered cities, and note that in 1969 one of the hundred delegates was African-American. Filed in Drive as "books/Schragger and Retzloff 2022 - The Failure of Home Rule Reform in Virginia.pdf", the journal's own PDF saved 30 September 2026}
