      \field{annotation}{The 1951 ruling, reported three days after it was handed down on Tuesday 20 March. The Board won. Judge Walter T. McCarthy "ruled Tuesday that appointment of Arlington department heads rests with the County Board", on a petition for a declaratory judgment filed by Commonwealth's Attorney Denman T. Hucker "after the Board, by resolution in January, reclaimed the appointive power from the manager". The Board, represented by Malcolm D. Miller and Benjamin M. Hedrick, had the manager law read for it: "After establishment of the county manager system here and until 1937, the Board appointed the department heads. In 1937 it delegated that authority to the county manager. The Board had no right to relinquish its power in 1937, Judge McCarthy said in his ruling, declaring a change in procedure could be accomplished only through an amendment to the county manager act." The police chief was outside the ruling: "The opposing attorneys had agreed that appointment of a police chief was not at issue, since a special legislative act gives this appointment to the manager. Judge McCarthy did not rule on that question in view of the agreement." That settles which way the ruling went, which dailysun1952petitions and dailysun1951policechief report in different words: it went for the Board on department heads and left the manager's hold on the police chief to the special act. Only the article is filed, not the page. Filed in Drive as "press/Sun/Sun 1951 - Board Wins Ruling Affirming Power to Appoint Heads of Departments (23 March 1951, p. 1).pdf", the article's text as Virginia Chronicle serves it at f=XML, set out on a plain page by code/sources/pages.py with plain OCR slips corrected and printed on 2 October 2026; the site refuses an automated request and its page images are behind a login, so no copy of the image was had.}
